  "ai_overview": {
    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "ACE inhibitors and ARBs (Angiotensin II Receptor Blockers) are equally effective first-line medications for lowering blood pressure, but ARBs are increasingly favored due to a significantly better safety profile.",
        "snippet_links": [
          {
            "text": "ACE inhibitors",
            "link": "https://www.goodrx.com/conditions/hypertension/ace-inhibitors-vs-arbs-whats-the-difference"
          }
        ],
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "ARBs are increasingly favored due to a significantly better safety profile"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Effectiveness"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Blood Pressure Lowering: Both drug classes lower blood pressure equally well by targeting the renin-angiotensin system (RAAS).",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Cardiovascular Outcomes: Large studies show no major difference between the two in preventing heart attacks, strokes, or heart failure hospitalizations."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Mechanism: ACE inhibitors (names ending in -pril) stop the production of angiotensin II. ARBs (names ending in -sartan ) block angiotensin II from binding to its receptors."
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          3
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Side Effects and Safety"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "ACE Inhibitors: Frequently cause a persistent, dry, hacking cough (in up to 20% of patients) and carry a rare but serious risk of angioedema (swelling of the face, lips, or tongue). This happens because ACE inhibitors cause a build-up of bradykinin .",
            "reference_indexes": [
              4,
              5,
              6
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "ARBs: Do not affect bradykinin levels, making the dry cough and angioedema extremely rare."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Shared Risks: Both can cause high potassium levels (hyperkalemia), low blood pressure, or kidney injury, and both are strictly contraindicated during pregnancy."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Clinical Recommendations"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "First-Choice Shift: While older guidelines historically listed ACE inhibitors first with ARBs as a backup, modern clinical reviews and large-scale data analyses recommend ARBs as the preferred first-line choice because patients tolerate them much better.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              7,
              8
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "No Combination: You should never take an ACE inhibitor and an ARB together, as this raises the risk of severe side effects like high potassium and kidney failure without extra benefit."
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          9
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you're deciding between these medications, let me know:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Do you have any history of a dry cough or other drug allergies?"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Are you managing other conditions like chronic kidney disease or diabetes?"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "I can help you discuss the right option with your doctor."
      }
    ],
